    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d177">

                <pb facs="0183" n="173" xml:id="pg173"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.24/1075: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Grew</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1941-06-06T12:00:00+09:00">June 6,
                            1941—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received June 7—3:30 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>773. Embassy’s 770, June 5, 8 p.m.<anchor n="31" xml:id="d177-f1"/> American
                    cargo at Haiphong.</p>
                <p>My French colleague made strong representations to the Japanese Government in a
                    first person note dated May 31, 1941 protesting that the agreement of August 30,
                        1940<anchor n="32" xml:id="d177-f2"/> and the assurances recently given him
                    by the Japanese Minister for Foreign Affairs do not give the Japanese military
                    the right of confiscation and seizure in Indochinese territory, the sovereign
                    rights of France to such territory being a monopoly. The note points out the
                    responsibility of the French Government, as the sovereign power, toward the
                    foreign owners of the goods at Haiphong and states that the French Government
                    cannot accept the Japanese proposal that these goods be transferred out of
                    Indochinese territory. The note, however, proposes that an arrangement be made
                    between the two governments for the establishment of one or more depots in
                    Indochina where these goods could be stored under joint Franco-Japanese control
                    “offering every guaranty of security”.</p>
                <p>Translation of note will go forward by mail on June 14.<anchor n="33"
                        xml:id="d177-f3"/>
                </p>
                <p>Sent to the Department via Shanghai.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Grew</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
